# r = n − d, THE REMAINDER VECTOR, WITH ITS SIGN DERIVED AND ITS EXPOSURE MEASURED.
# One row per layer that states BOTH a demand and a nameplate, so both operands exist.
# The key is (filing, layer) and every downstream fit question reads this relation.
#
# ⛔⛔ SUBTRACTING TWO INTERVALS REVERSES THE BOUNDS. The low of n − d pairs n's LOW
#    against d's HIGH, because the smallest remainder is the smallest supply meeting the
#    largest demand. Get it backwards and every remainder in the corpus comes out inside
#    out while every individual number still looks plausible.
#
# ⭐⭐ `derived_fit` IS A COMPARISON OF TWO RANGES AND NOT OF TWO POINTS -- ISO 286's own
#    criterion, borrowed intact. `clearance` = supply exceeds demand ACROSS THE WHOLE RANGE.
#    `interference` = demand exceeds supply across the whole range. `transition` = the
#    ranges overlap, so which one wins depends on where in the range you land. That third
#    case is the one a two-valued encoding cannot name, and the corpus contains it.
#
# ⭐ `exposure` IS WHAT THE FILING'S OWN NUMBERS SAY COULD HAVE GONE UNSERVED: the largest
#   demand against the smallest supply, floored at zero. It is not a measurement anybody
#   made; it is the ceiling their two measurements imply, which is why the rule built on
#   it compares against what they ADMIT went unserved rather than against a reading.
#
# ⛔ THIS RELATION WAS ONCE WRITTEN THREE TIMES AND DRIFTED -- twice inside one file, under
#   one name, with different column lists, so a coverage table reported on a `base` that
#   was not the `base` the rules ran against. It is one file now and both readings compose it.
-- from pm.layer and pm.nameplate; pm:Layer/pm:Remainder/sign carries the filed classification.
SELECT d.filing, d.layer,
       l.sign, l.sign_absent,
       l.absorber_taxonomy, l.absorber_value,
       d.d_low, d.d_mode, d.d_high, d.d_unit AS unit,
       n.n_low, n.n_mode, n.n_high, n.n_unit AS amount_unit,
       n.n_low  - d.d_high AS r_low,   -- crossed: the low of n − d pairs n.low with d.HIGH
       n.n_mode - d.d_mode AS r_mode,
       n.n_high - d.d_low  AS r_high,
       CASE WHEN n.n_low  - d.d_high >= 0 THEN 'clearance'
            WHEN n.n_high - d.d_low  <= 0 THEN 'interference'
            ELSE 'transition' END AS derived_fit,
       greatest(d.d_high - n.n_low, 0) AS exposure,
       n.lumpy, n.quantum_mode, n.quantum_unit
FROM      (
    :compose(layers/demand.sqlc)
) d
JOIN      (
    :compose(layers/nameplate.sqlc)
) n USING (filing, layer)
JOIN pm.layer l USING (filing, layer)
